#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180497} :activity/id #uuid "ff0789e0-f094-5fc5-ac25-230d7c590b14" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049147 :topic/id #uuid "de74c316-f607-5e04-8847-2af8b65a611b" :topic/math-academy-id 3694} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180493 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179739 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179601 :question/id #uuid "d5a9cb43-6396-5153-a60c-3d653d61a47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-9536"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dab6bfb9-608d-5ff8-88e7-5aa9612acda4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049853}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180017} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259813} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259814} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259816} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275256} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551246}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Constant Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "0c7bbec7-4f05-52c6-86b2-ee55d5eec5cf" :step/math-academy-id 35727 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180503}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180497 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180720 :tutorial/content "Suppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ and $C$ is a smooth curve in the $xy$-plane. Recall that the line integral\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\nrepresents the total work done by $\\mathbf F$ in moving a particle along $C.$ Loosely speaking:\n\n- if $W > 0,$ then $\\mathbf F$ causes the particle to *speed up* as it moves along $C,$ and\n\n- if $W < 0,$ then $\\mathbf F$ causes the particle to *slow down* as it moves along $C.$\n\nTo understand this, consider the vector field $\\mathbf F$ and the path $C$ defined by the line segment $\\overline{AB},$ shown below. \n\n![](images/2a/2a3b3896767e5f6beb4eec39f44afcc2387e178df0cec0ebbbdaa5e4cb68e964.png)\n\n \n\nIt's helpful to imagine that $\\mathbf F$ represents a uniform gravitational field near the earth's surface. \n\nLet's consider two examples.\n\n- If a particle moves from $A$ to $B$ along the segment, it will pick up speed because it moves *with* the vector field:\n\n![](images/0f/0f0bbc24f7ed26390643546bf578b88a879da58f56ac0b817159deeea25f164b.png)\n\n \nIntuitively, this makes sense. We know from real-world experience that if we drop a pebble, its speed increases as it descends. \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r > 0.\n$$\n\n- If a particle moves from $B$ to $A$ along the segment, it will slow down because it moves *against* the vector field:\n\n![](images/2e/2eabe985ee8b360ef79841561547b2251a818e63e53e91785a83d0c2cf3609e3.png)\n\nAgain, this matches our intuition: If we toss a pebble into the air, its speed decreases as it ascends.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nW = \\int\\limits_{C}\\mathbf F\\cdot\\textrm d \\mathbf r < 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "2e08c55c-9b68-57ce-97fe-81922244739a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5834 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2c33a62f-70ce-5226-b2d4-39e1df8540da" :step/math-academy-id 35733 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180502}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180498 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179742 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179604 :question/id #uuid "d844a573-49a1-59c3-bec4-e409c486469d" :question/math-academy-id "e-9542"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3d72127a-90d6-5f7e-bd87-75d03285a918" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050885}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180019} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180020} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259817} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259818} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259820} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259844} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275259} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535015} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535078} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551240}] :knowledge-point/title "Interpreting the Sign of a Line Integral Over a Non-Constant Vector Field"} :step/id #uuid "e76a04e3-a2f3-5a0a-9547-3a509b4d4d5c" :step/math-academy-id 35747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180502 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180723 :tutorial/content "In some situations, a particle's speed increases and then decreases (or vice-versa) as it moves along a curve in a vector field. \n\nLet's consider a particle moving from $A$ to $B$ along the arc of a semicircle centered at $O$ in a uniform gravitational field, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/466bd1716e626f6e3cc7859589974c27d49bdaa77bed9aa5813ef4bd9f3328e9.png)\n\n \n\nNote that:\n\n- as the particle moves from $A$ to $T,$ it works against $\\mathbf F$ and slows down, and\n\n- as the particle moves from $T$ to $B,$ it works with $\\mathbf F$ and speeds up.\n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ for $x <                   0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x > 0.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r =     0.\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this means that the particle's speed is the same at the points $A$ and $B,$ and these are the points of maximum speed. \n\nAdditionally, since $\\displaystyle\\int_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r$ is decreasing as we move from $A$ to $T,$ the particle moves at its slowest speed at the point $T.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f659a6f3-f2aa-5170-a3d7-e5ba24fd59c0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5843 :tutorial/title "Cases When a Particle Experiences Acceleration and Deceleration"} :step/id #uuid "dda3ff66-7674-5b97-99fb-85a94a2b066a" :step/math-academy-id 35802 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180493}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180503 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180724 :tutorial/content "Another noteworthy situation is when ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) is perpendicular to ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) at all points along ![](images/27/278f99df1e14fe0ebfb61bdb72262a62670dcaa60d322148bc3ee591b278ec2d.svg)\n\n![](images/f3/f3f8b96e06c9e802e038130db69356924df9c9a385dc913e55cb5d9cc3a697b1.png)\n\n \n\nSince the force ![](images/c7/c75d4b37611dd632920692d430870b4d582fed06ac19178ccf69eb7c7dcb264a.svg) acts perpendicular to the particle's motion as it moves from ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) to ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) it neither helps nor hinders its motion. Therefore,\n\n![](images/06/06b0f05fc417c4572bb7763607ef1d471efc1282067b22c372f23caba33fcb19.svg)\n\nIn particular, if ![](images/c0/c0c348883f19c18a48444415da35aeaa27f8a18606bf5955e34aec416e248cad.svg) is a level curve of a scalar field ![](images/bc/bc562d54615dad55d095db2afed7626f62ff7ebf0ab6c5d45471b77ac30b6398.svg) and ![](images/c5/c5b03af80a954012283edf6f027c02c7fe387d08ee6424e6b92186d580a65e7e.svg) then\n![](images/b3/b3dddb4aa6f178a9ab5ec8245e4881b260d60cfba33123b56b8b5ea99cda95d8.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "13e79581-4a65-5db7-9362-f065ba606386" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5844 :tutorial/title "Cases When the Vector Field and Curve are Perpendicular"} :step/id #uuid "51bd906d-35f0-5627-ad2d-ac5987f58545" :step/math-academy-id 35803 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180498}}] :activity/title "Interpreting Line Integrals of Vector-Valued Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186179848}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551387} :activity/id #uuid "d303828c-4774-564e-856f-3e961776778d" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049147 :topic/id #uuid "de74c316-f607-5e04-8847-2af8b65a611b" :topic/math-academy-id 3694} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259845 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "77d82670-955d-5fbd-bc9e-ff9b5595ca19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463536} {:answer/id #uuid "70953006-86df-56ba-885e-a59756ac68a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463537}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463536} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bbc6ae6-a433-51b7-9bcc-4234a55196ba" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464702} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3413fb0-066c-5f24-a376-7d72fef132fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463538} {:answer/id #uuid "090b215e-b754-554a-8109-db90d4abfb27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463539}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463539} :answer-field/id #uuid "79b6f686-1017-5c39-b085-1d1f73f3ac2e" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464703} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3c62597-d021-5cbd-8c81-864877afd3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463540} {:answer/id #uuid "9d291d8d-f31c-5932-8efc-6cd6f690f503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186463541}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186463540} :answer-field/id #uuid "162bbeb9-8561-5809-b4fd-17fcbbfe7d72" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186464704}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e9d575c8-adec-5386-bb2f-10bc638decac" :question/math-academy-id "q-125706" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a423ab56220e438fb849d2025ab53b99bb74ef598e16110c911d691680130527.png)\n\nConsider the semicircle and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works  against the particle for $x<0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x>0.$ \n\n![](images/cd/cd52e91c72d481980c9a48575da7ca3eaebd2ed27f96b1054cf5c1c5da1aa3d9.png)\n\n \nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So, \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a1/a1e8fa5262fc787b691b3753fc57ab39aceee7c57dbbe166c6da6f6effaf42dc.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "1917119d-a3c7-5df2-a185-1f304577335a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551389}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551387 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180020 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47df525a-0d27-5474-842e-e797864d924f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459479} {:answer/id #uuid "0f4b12aa-dfc8-5cf6-bb46-fa30cda83363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459480}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459479} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ebe448c-6d78-52eb-b87a-c0f6378a526d" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460606} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b3a5163-4335-5da1-a308-4afc6fa83343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459481} {:answer/id #uuid "2865bf65-f4fa-5527-9338-dac5b94be08d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459482}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459482} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6785c8-4fa8-5419-98ef-b6ca891fd2fe" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460607} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a7f457e-5e87-5921-9c97-354280faa2c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459483} {:answer/id #uuid "58d82791-fb0b-511f-a411-3a2ed07c51ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186459484}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186459484} :answer-field/id #uuid "5480c90b-6e84-5672-a102-83d8323aaae1" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186460608}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "26c0cce0-6deb-59c6-a9c4-06e4698080d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-125381" :question/problem "![](images/50/501a3512fb4b8a1898c6dfb8816d587de4a0cd585991d9c92d2bb35d83a2853d.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y)$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the semicircle is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is false while statement II is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ works against the motion of the particle as it moves from $A$ to $B.$\n\n![](images/4c/4c11b38f9e598e0cd8fc50715d2091e5bea48d188440ae8a99c84d66a6acb6b9.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. It's clear from the picture below that $F$ assists the motion of the particle as it moves from $B$ to $A.$\n\n![](images/34/346007ae582fccdf4519a3d1cd50a467e19d5345238343a1311fd3348bbeb138.png)\n\n \nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "3551427b-781d-572b-8a50-737fe5febecf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551425}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551389 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414783 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "740bd403-f1e6-5a92-a2b9-efa1ee0f552f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472742} {:answer/id #uuid "5170cfef-7833-5d81-bf15-5bd1c2583c78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472743}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472742} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ab5fa05-0008-5864-bfbd-37a853fa3a21" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473766} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13931620-6744-5279-819c-a867d3d2f624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472744} {:answer/id #uuid "89f7ef7d-3d29-5bcd-86e4-6ccb6a042395" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472745}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472745} :answer-field/id #uuid "0562aeab-6992-5c67-82d7-eee722fd8045" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "194dcf31-6ded-5f7d-8ad4-8503fc14610b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472746} {:answer/id #uuid "cff66d04-5359-528e-90cc-f15b10b85371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472747}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186472746} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cfac123-c198-5997-bc78-bc4c79e3be46" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0b65bfe0-6178-564e-825d-19d4ac9443d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-150012" :question/problem "![](images/5a/5a3961346a4bcb0d94e46caf6d6ed1253dc56fb4761db07b11829e329bc2fa8d.png)\n\nConsider the part of parabola and constant vector field $F$ shown above. Let $C$ be the path where the part of parabola is traversed from $A$ to $B.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n- ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0$\n- ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Remember that ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ gives the total work done by a force $F$ in moving a particle along $C:$\n\n- If the force works *with* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force helps to push the particle along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r>0.$\n\n- If the force works *against* the motion of the particle (i.e., the force impedes the motion of the particle as it moves along the path), then ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r<0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statements I is false, while statement II is true. As the particle moves from $A$ to $B,$ the force $F$ works against the particle for $x>0,$ while $F$  works with the particle for $x<0.$ \n\n![](images/af/af80eab6427f376084301c03715fd098cfe4a1c816dcd72d3e1129cfa35ff29d.png)\n\n \n\nBy symmetry, the contribution to ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r$ for $x<0$ is canceled out by the contribution for $x>0.$ So,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. If we reverse the orientation of $C,$ then the situation with regards to the work done by $F$ is similar to the previous case.\n\n![](images/a2/a26ca79400bf30aa891543d30a052a6e8ec071ccb48fcd8c418fe0a713dad212.png)\n\n \n\nSo, ${\\int }_{-C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "398a54a2-4081-54b9-91fa-06fdfcf9e9d3"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551425 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ed18d44-37e8-529c-a585-0ceab88f3f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550685} {:answer/id #uuid "8858c48e-583e-5419-89c1-66b239d5970c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550713} {:answer/id #uuid "b2545c5c-af10-54e4-a998-61fc386ed756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186550854} {:answer/id #uuid "d8